  "classification_limits": [
    "Only exact built-in OSError types with errno EACCES or EPERM become os_permission. The report omits WinError, operation, traceback and error context.",
    "The counter is one failed checkpoint attempt, not one failed native receipt. It is never cleared by a later successful checkpoint.",
    "Checkpoint errors retain pending committed identities for a later checkpoint; native receipt DB persistence is not replayed by this path.",
    "Zero receipt_durable_pending does not by itself establish zero command-activity checkpoint backlog or clear sticky degraded state.",
    "The recorded stats are captured before daemon.stop; a later shutdown failure cannot have caused this already captured event.",
    "The broad catch covers lock acquisition and release, aggregate and preview I/O, replacement and cleanup. A cleanup exception can mask an earlier operation in existing finally blocks; terminal errno alone does not exclude that chain."
  ],
  "source_exclusions": [
    "Windows fsync_directory returns without opening a directory.",
    "Checkpoint closes its aggregate read descriptor and temporary write descriptor before os.replace. Preview reader likewise closes before sidecar mutation.",
    "The new private-state windows_atomic_replace helper is not called by these journal functions.",
    "All exposed normal journal read/append/rewrite paths use the same lock; a legitimate second participating reader should not hold the aggregate file while checkpoint replaces it after admission."
  ],
  "unproved_hypotheses": [
    "The Windows lock path calls os.ftruncate(fd,1) before acquiring its byte-zero lock; another holder may make this a concrete contention boundary, but current artifact does not identify it.",
    "An unexpected outside holder or filesystem policy may affect aggregate/sidecar replace or cleanup; no actual holder has been attributed."
  ],
  "next_evidence": "A failure-only fixed code-identity/line operation capture around the existing writer classifier can locate the terminal OS failure without touching successful checkpoint operations. Retain bounded exception context so cleanup masking remains explicit.",
